Let's see...100K users, 2K document, 30 seconds. That's a theoretical peak
load of 200MB/30sec,
or 1600Mb/30sec, or 53 Mb/sec. You can get that down an OC3 pipe, but
where's it all going to
go once it hits your ISP?

Think "server farm". Look into the Cisco Local Director and something like
a bunch of Sun
Ultra 2's behind it. That approach will do wonders for your expected
availability, too.

John M. Gibby wrote:

> I am researching hardware and software suggestions for a super web
> server that can support 100,000 web users at one time downloading a 2k
> (average) HTML document with a response time of no more than 30 seconds
> for each user.  This server would probably be connecting through a
> T3/OC3 (If you have any suggestions on connection "pipes", I would
> appreciate them as well.)
>
> Any help will be greatly appreciated.  Thanks for your time & Merry
