> 
> Hi
> 
> I'm posting this from home PC, it appears my DEC 3000/300 has crashed!
> This is the second time in a fortnight that this has happened, the
> only recent change was a Firmware upgrade, in preparation to upgrade
> to DUnix 4.0B (I'm on 4.0 at the moment).  I've had the system 3 years
> and this is only the second time its crashed!  Last time it crashed I
> could find no clue as to what had happened, the only way I could find
> out when it crashed was looking at the mail logs.  When I sat down in
> front of the machine, all I got was the PROM prompt.  Booting up and
> looking in logs didn't tell me anything, though I had no trouble
> booting as far as I can recall.

Look in the /var/adm/crash directory for the output of the 'crashdc' 
cmd and the output of 'savecore'. You should additionally use the 'uerf' 
cmd to check out the binary error file.

> 
> I would like some info on how to find out EXACTLY what has gone wrong,
> so what do I do in order to do things to maximize my chance of finding
> out?  Boot first to signle user?  Is there any point to this?  Which
> commands to run first?

This will minimize the chance that you will crash while investigated and
will allow you to run the 'savecore' cmd manually, but if you have
already 
booted the machine to multi-user state and have a /sbin/swapdefault file 
or have used the system enough to have used the swap space then the dump
is possibly already corrupted and a manual 'savecore' will fail. 

Charlie

