steve.kappel@iname.com (Steve Kappel) writes:
> You aren't going to find a better platform than a Sun when you
> consider performance and cost.  At the very high end I suppose
--

I would disagree on both points you make.

A large web server is going to be limited by the total I/O bus
bandwidth, the total number of spindles and the total network
bandwidth. Also, most versions of UNIX only support 65536 file
descriptors per process. Furthermore, Web traffic is easily
partitioned across multiple machines.

If you have a single multithreaded system, you will either be running
multiple instances of a single (non-threaded) web server or a single
instance of a threaded web server. In either case, a large shared
memory machine will suffer from locking and contention, either in the
O/S or the application.

I would think you really want a large collection of very fast
single-processor systems connected by a reasonable switch (fast
ethernet would probably do). This way, for N systems, you can get N
SCSI channels (or 3N if you use the right controllers) with ~8N disks,
2N network channels, no O/S locking and N*65536 file descriptors.  You
would need to use a hashing function to chop up the URL space and
delegate the requests, but this is simple, and you'd need to do it for
a single large system anyway, because the file descriptor limit
implies you'l need to run multiple web servers.

You want the fastest single-processor system you could get that
supports UltraSCSI. DEC alpha 164PC's or Pentium-II 300's running
Linux wouldn't be a bad bet, since you could probably assemble a box
with 4 disks, ethernet, 128MB and single ultraSCSI controller for
<$5K. I suggested Linux because it has good single-processor
performance and a smaller memory footprint than e.g., Solaris or
Digital Unix.



-- 
Dirk Grunwald                 Assoc. Prof, Univ. of Colorado at Boulder



