I generally agree with your statements.

However, the thread (of late at least) has been about single systems
or at least mostly single "image" systems.  As with anything there
is no free lunch - the farm of individual machines you suggest
has additional administrative overhead.

There has been a lot of activity surrounding people consolidating
many servers into fewer - part of the reason for the huge success
of the E10000.  Many situations do not have the technical expertise
to operate a farm.

The farm approach is also a poor solution for web *applications*
that are purely dynamic (ordering systems, for example).  Working
at the front lines in this area I see a massive movement beyond
the pretty static pages that lend themselves to a farm towards
essentially high-volume OLTP applications.  Sure, one could have
a single database engine driven by a farm of application servers
but I find no joy in the idea that I would have to maintain such
a setup.


