>>>>> In article <348DA49A.41C6@mit.edu>, "F. Joseph Pompei" <pompei!!NOSPAM!!@mit.edu> writes:

> Hey folks, I'm having trouble using dxladebug or ladebug on my Alpha
> 500 (OSF1 V4.0 564.32 alpha).  When my compiled code is loaded (and
> presumably run) for a debug, my *entire* OS crashes, forcing a
> reboot. This seems to happen no matter what I try to debug.. it's a
> bit baffling, and very troubling.  If this is an OS bug, it's a
> semi-serious security issue as well.

> 	Has anyone seen this before?
Hi Yes,
this happened to me too.  You need to patch the kernel; someone put 
some instructions on:

http://www-archive.stanford.edu/lists/alpha-osf-managers/hyper97/1094.html

which worked for my machine fine. (2a and 2b were enough).


Cheers, Stephen



1) Compile a new kernel with no KDEBUG option

2) Modify the current kernel:

2.a) For this session only (this means that next time you
reboot the bug will turn on again

dbx -k /vmunix
p db_enable

if db_enable is 1 you have the bug

assign db_enable = 0

2.b) Permanent modification

As in step 2.a for current session +

dbx /vmunix (we need the disk image)

patch db_enable = 0

This patches the disk image of the executable, so no
kernel rebuild is required.



